Project "ENTRAGATE ERP" {
  database_type: 'PostgreSQL'
  Note: "Canonical persistence contract for ENTRAGATE ERP (ADR-0024 baseline v2). 20 Postgres schemas for 22 modules: 20 modules own one schema each (product and warehouse were split out of inventory by ADR-0026), `auth` is shared by the `user` and `organization` modules because Better Auth owns it, and the `webhook` module owns no schema at all. Module isolation is a hard rule: a Ref may only connect tables owned by the same schema, and every module owns exactly one schema. Cross-module links are nullable logical id columns plus a column note (ADR-0014), never a foreign key. Datetimes are unix milliseconds in bigint. Money is exact integer IDR minor units in bigint plus a currency_code column. Tenant scope is organization_id (text, references auth.organizations.id logically). Soft delete is deleted_at (bigint, nullable) on every business table except append-only ledgers and projections, which document their exemption in their table note. Status vocabularies are text guarded by Checks blocks, never native enums. Document numbering is one `<schema>.code_numberings` table per schema that allocates codes (ADR-0024 Q6) - there is no shared code_number schema. Partial unique indexes for soft-deleted codes are implemented directly in the Drizzle schema files, which DBML cannot express. Composite tenant foreign keys are not used: the tenant match of a row and the rows it references is enforced by the owning module's resolve/assert contract methods. Better Auth owned tables (auth.users, auth.sessions, auth.accounts, auth.verifications, auth.organizations, auth.members, auth.invitations, auth.passkeys, auth.apikeys) are the exception to the soft-delete rule: only auth.users and auth.organizations carry deleted_at, the rest are hard deleted by Better Auth itself, so their tables must never gain a deleted_at column nor be queried with one. Tables marked `status: target` in their Note are listed by the owner but not built yet (ADR-0024 Q22)."
}

